<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="de933385">&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The amount of data available for testing is often determined by&amp;nbsp;constraints such as time, cost and quality
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="eeef3cb9"></attribute>
<attribute name="usageGuidance" nodeId="ef339774"></attribute>
<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="3d6badac">&lt;p&gt;
    Test data can be obtained in various ways. Commonly used approaches for obtaining test data include:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Harvesting production data
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Utilize data generation tools (e.g., Optim, Informatica, Ab Initio, EMS, Advance)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Utilize the application under test itself&amp;nbsp;to generate test data in an automated process, if possible
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Once test data is obtained,&amp;nbsp;it is loaded into the appropriate test environment. Consult with stakeholders for
    documentation for loading test data as processes vary from one organization to the next.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
